Psoriasis Severe Plaque with Systemic Fatigue
Complex Fatigue Entities
Cutaneous autoimmune disease with systemic burden

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-041
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of rapidly spreading, painful dermatological plaques and profound immobility due to systemic exhaustion.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, explosive worsening of widespread cutaneous inflammation accompanied by severe clinical fatigue, preventing the patient from standing or performing basic hygiene. Immediate on-site assessment was mandated to rule out acute generalized pustular psoriasis or superimposed severe bacterial cellulitis, and to provide potent anti-inflammatory rescue prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My skin is cracking and burning everywhere, and I am so deeply exhausted I can't even sit up in bed.
History of Present Illness: Patient with established psoriasis reports a severe 48-hour exacerbation of both cutaneous and systemic symptoms following a period of high psychosocial stress. Symptoms include rapid expansion of thick scaly plaques across the torso and extensor surfaces, which are now actively fissuring and bleeding. Accompanying this is widespread inflammation causing severe skin tightness, and a crushing, clinical fatigue that the patient describes as a "full body battery drain."
Functional Status: Bedbound; unable to tolerate clothing or position changes due to skin fissuring and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of secondary bacterial infection due to open fissures; extremely reliant on spouse for hydration and topical care tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.4 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, keeping limbs slightly flexed to avoid stretching cracked plaques.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals extensive, thick, erythematous plaques with dense silvery scale covering approximately 35 percent of total body surface area. Widespread inflammation is evident with several active, bleeding fissures on the elbows and knees. No localized fluctuance, honey-colored crusting, or red streaking suggestive of acute bacterial cellulitis. Joint exam reveals no active synovitis. Presentation is classic for severe cutaneous autoimmune disease with systemic burden.
Environmental Observations: Bed linens spotted with serosanguinous exudate. Room temperature elevated, worsening pruritus.
Medication Reconciliation: Exhausted supply of high-potency topical steroids 3 days ago. No current systemic immunosuppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Psoriasis Severe Plaque with Systemic Fatigue - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Generalized Pustular Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of widespread, sterile, pinpoint pustules coalescing into lakes of pus, and lack of high-grade fever.

Acute Cellulitis (Superimposed) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Diffuse, bilateral distribution of erythema rather than a single, rapidly expanding unilateral hot area, and normal leukocyte temperature profile.

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of punched-out, painful vesicles or rapid clinical deterioration out of proportion to typical plaque morphology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of rapidly fissuring, painful plaques and immobility mandated urgent physical assessment to explicitly rule out life-threatening dermatological emergencies such as generalized pustular psoriasis or severe secondary sepsis. Having confirmed an explosive autoimmune plaque flare driven by medication non-compliance, urgent re-initiation of potent anti-inflammatory therapies was required to halt the active skin cracking and reduce the massive systemic inflammatory burden that was causing the patient's profound, immobilizing fatigue.

